  "body": "The best error UX in the family was already built. It was just never printed.\n\nEvery `CoordError` carries four facts beyond its code and reason \u2014 the purpose of the boundary that refused, at least two concrete fixes, a narrow next step, and a repository-relative docs anchor. `repair_metadata` fills all four for every code, every anchor resolves in `docs/errors.md`, and a test already asserted there are never fewer than two fixes. The only consumers were four assertions in the test suite, because `Display` writes `{code}: {reason}` and nothing read the rest.\n\n## Before\n\n```\nbullet-family: UNSUPPORTED_SCHEMA: family.lock schema 2 is not installable; retain it for diagnosis, regenerate schema 3 from authenticated signed tags, and replace it atomically\n```\n\n## After\n\n```\nbullet-family: UNSUPPORTED_SCHEMA\n  family.lock schema 2 is not installable; retain it for diagnosis, regenerate schema 3 from authenticated signed tags, and replace it atomically\n\n  protecting    reject a schema outside the admitted version set\n  try           - follow the explicit export or removal procedure\n                - use the binary and lock version that own the subject\n  next          follow the schema-removal or setup-recovery runbook\n  docs          docs/errors.md#unsupported-or-corrupt-state\n```\n\n## What is deliberately not changed\n\n`Display` is untouched and stays `{code}: {reason}`. It is the machine contract, and a lot of tooling greps stderr for that shape. The rendered form still leads with the code on line one so those greps keep working on it too.\n\n**The reason text is never hard-wrapped.** Wrapping it broke `release_profiles::ordinary_release_requires_an_explicit_profile`, which greps for a phrase that a newline had landed inside. That failure was the useful kind \u2014 it identifies exactly which text other people depend on. A terminal soft-wraps a long line for free, so only the metadata block, which nothing greps because until now it was never printed, is laid out to a width.\n\n## One colour rule, no new dependency\n\n`src/diagnostic.rs` decides colour once, from values passed in rather than looked up, so the rule is a pure function with tests:\n\n| Input | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `NO_COLOR` present and non-empty | never paint |\n| `CLICOLOR_FORCE` non-empty and not `0` | paint even a pipe |\n| `TERM` `dumb` or absent | never paint, even when forced |\n| otherwise | the depth the terminal advertises |\n\nThree depth tiers \u2014 basic, 256, truecolor \u2014 because a palette that emits truecolor unconditionally is a bet, not a palette. Six named roles, so no single value quietly acquires a second meaning. `--no-color`, `--plain` and `--color=<auto|always|never>` are new, and are stripped from argv before dispatch since colour is a property of the terminal rather than of any command.\n\n## USAGE\n\nThe 985-character single-line constant is now a grouped listing. `tests/family_lock.rs` asserted on one concatenated fragment; that is now two positive assertions and one negative, which is stricter than what it replaced \u2014 it would catch one verb's arguments drifting while the other's did not.\n\n## Cost paid\n\nTest inventory 600 \u2192 616 hub, 819 \u2192 835 total, with both identity digests regenerated by running `ops/ci/test-partitions.sh` rather than by editing numbers. 16 new tests, no skipped or ignored test.\n\nProved on the development host: the whole `fast` lane green at 616/616, and every new test failing when the behaviour it describes is removed.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_015qHQABNS382h2gi5jb5oUY\n",
